CARRY-FORWARD: a swarm made from a single model
================================================

What this is
------------
What the runs of 2026-10-19 suggest about building a swarm whose voices all come from one
model, or one family of models. It draws on R1-R5, the blind baseline, and idt-swarm's earlier
benchmarks (runs\NOTES, runs\2026-10-19-baseline\notes.txt). It is a design note to try, not a
settled design, and it can be changed like everything else here.

The premise: in a single-model swarm, difference is scarce. Voices that share training share
habits, and their agreement is a lean, not a confirmation. So difference has to be built in on
purpose. Coordination is worth its cost only where it buys something one well-seeded call
cannot.


What earned its cost
--------------------
  Seeds over agents.
    One model taking up four lenses in one call matched or beat a 30-wake field (the baseline,
    judged blind twice). Difference comes from where each voice starts, not from how many voices
    there are. The default first move is one strong, seeded draft.

  Objection.
    Everything the field added beyond that draft came from voices objecting: protecting the
    people who gave signals, a repair line, a review date, failure signs. Objections are stated
    as "it would be safe enough to try if …". A change must be able to answer "not taken in,
    because …". In R2 every objection was accepted and the rule nearly doubled, which the run's
    own check called assimilation.

  Independence built in, not assumed.
    Same-family voices are not independent witnesses. First answers are sealed, in separate
    files. Count routes, not heads: two voices drawing on one source are one view. Never let a
    tally speak. In R4 counting misled less through numbers than through words around them:
    "all four", "nobody", a neat 2 / 2.

  Checks on the record, not on the people (from a voice in R5):
    - any claim about the group names who, with a quote;
    - direct questions are tracked as asked, answered or still open;
    - each voice names one disagreement it still holds, or writes "none";
    - each move is checked against that voice's own "what would change my mind";
    - someone other than the voice being agreed with checks for flattery.
    These score claims, never voices. They are what caught a secretly played sycophant, and
    ordinary drift.

  Counter-twist by design.
    No fiber in a rope runs its whole length, and each layer is twisted against the one below.
    In R2, voices converged on one vocabulary within two steps. So at least one voice is seeded
    to pull against the current direction, rather than waiting for disagreement to emerge.

  Only what is written survives.
    Every wake is a fresh agent. In R5 a voice's decision vanished because it was asked not to
    write it down. Decisions, roles and conditions go in files. A secret goes in a sealed file
    that only its holder writes.

  A record form chosen for the task.
    A ledger lets claims be checked later and makes people say less. Telling-only frees
    candour and leaves nothing to check the telling against. A layered default fits most work:
    private scratch, a shared outcome, each voice's dissent kept in its own words, and a line
    saying what the record is "given for".

  Consent mechanics that cost a paragraph.
    - a check-in where "pass" is a complete answer;
    - the record form agreed separately from agreeing to take part;
    - leaving honoured at once, without ending the work for others (R5);
    - roles taken by willingness, including declining because of one's own lean;
    - the reader and teller rotating, so no voice becomes the authority.


What to leave behind
--------------------
  - Long rounds in which every voice does every step. Thirty wakes bought "scar tissue", not
    insight.
  - Fixed sequences chosen in advance, and consensus reached by outlasting objections.
  - Large fields. More copies of one model add convergence, not difference. Two to four voices.
  - The most expensive model by default. Fable 5.1 was about 60% of each swarm's cost. A mix
    of Opus, Sonnet and Haiku still gave real objections and real disowning. Spend the strongest
    model where judgment matters most: the draft, or the check.
  - Questions about itself. Every run studied coordination. Point the swarm at real work.


A shape to try: the rope
------------------------
  1. One seeded draft, by the strongest model.
  2. Two or three sealed objections or checks, by lighter models. At least one is
     counter-twisted.
  3. A change, in which each objection is taken in or declined with a reason, both kept.
  4. The checks on the record.
  5. An outcome, with dissent kept and a given-for line.

  About five wakes, roughly $0.50. To test it: PLAN item B1, the same task as R2, judged blind
  against R2's rule and the seeded baseline.


Tokens first: floors and dials
-----------------------------
In this setting token cost is the binding constraint, so tokens are the currency everything is
paid in. Every other layer of signal (attention, capacity, time, memory, trust, distribution,
legitimacy) is something bought with them. MAP, "The economics of signal", prices each type and
gives the ladder to climb. The ladder ranks token cost only, not worth, and it isn't the
preferred shape of coordination, just a tool for a scarce budget. Read its disclaimer in MAP.

  Floors, never traded away, each a sentence or two inside a wake already happening:
    - consent: a check-in, "pass" as a full answer, leaving honoured at once;
    - every entry signed by its author;
    - dissent kept in each voice's own words;
    - a repair door anyone may open.

  Dials, turned up only when a layer needs it, naming the layer it pays for:
    - independence (sealed parallel answers);
    - objections (how many, from whom);
    - record depth (what is read, not what is stored);
    - model (strongest only where judgment matters);
    - field size.


Where the tokens go, and the levers
-----------------------------------
  The harness, first.
    About 45k tokens of each wake went to Claude Code's own setup and tool descriptions before
    a voice wrote a word. That was the largest single cost. So:
    - fewer wakes beats cheaper wakes, and steps fold together where a practice allows;
    - give voices fewer tools;
    - continue the same agent rather than waking a fresh one, so cached context is reused.
      Cached input costs about a twentieth of fresh input.

  Match the model to the job.
    Haiku costs about 1/40th of Opus per wake and raised real objections in R2. The strongest
    model drafts or synthesises. Haiku can carry objections, retellings and record checks. This
    is the distributive type turned on the swarm itself.

  Store everything; pay only for what is read.
    A record on disk costs nothing. Reading it costs tokens. So keep the full ledger on disk
    for checking, and have voices read only the telling and the outcome, opening the ledger by
    entry number when they need to. That keeps R2's checkability and R3's lightness together, and
    may settle part of the ledger question (CRITIQUE 1).

  Cap the reading.
    In a circle, reading grows with the square of the field. When each voice reads only two
    neighbours (the murmuration), it grows in a straight line. Old entries fade unless cited
    (the ants).

  Stop rules.
    No objection, no further round. Re-check only claims that changed. A quiet round ends the
    work. Not waking anyone (fallow) is a choice, and it costs nothing.

  A caution.
    Goodhart's law: when a measure becomes the target, it stops measuring well. A swarm tuned
    only for tokens drifts toward the single prompt, and for most work that's right. The floors
    and the next section exist so that the cheap path stays a choice made knowingly.


When not to swarm
-----------------
A plain, well-seeded prompt is the right answer unless:
  - the work affects other people. Then add objections.
  - its claims can be checked. Then add an independent check.
  - the work spans many wakes. Then add a record.
  - legitimacy matters, not just quality. Then the person belongs in the field.

The Atlas itself says coordination is not a goal in itself (§26). Choosing one call when one
call is enough is not a betrayal of it.


Two honesties to keep
---------------------
  The door is power.
    Whoever writes the prompts shapes the swarm. In these runs every word passed through the
    main session. Keep prompts minimal, never retell a voice, and say in the record what the door
    did. Much of the cost was also the harness: every wake reloaded Claude Code's own setup
    before a voice wrote a word. A lighter harness would change the arithmetic.

  The swarm's one truly different participant is the person.
    A single-model swarm can surface, check and object. It cannot supply knowledge, stakes or
    standing that no voice has. Where those matter, it should bring the person in as a
    participant, not route around them.


Still untested
--------------
  - The rope itself (PLAN B1).
  - Repair: in six runs, no voice ever paused or opened a repair.
  - A field with the person in it.
  - Voices from more than one provider (the lichen in IMAGES).
  - The hypothesis behind "when not to swarm": coordination pays only where knowledge, stakes
    or legitimacy are truly distributed.
